---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { club } from "../lib/config.js";
import { blobsStore } from "../lib/store.js";

// The guide itself is club-neutral. The club's own tips come from the admin's venues page.
// Results pages link to the ids below, so keep them stable.
const { clubTips, chatUrl } = await blobsStore().getVenues();
---

<Base title="Rowing 101">
  <p class="eyebrow">New to rowing?</p>
  <h1>Rowing 101</h1>
  <p class="muted">
    A short guide to regattas and results for families new to the sport. Tap any underlined term on
    a results page to jump here.
  </p>

  <section class="card" id="races">
    <h2>Two kinds of race</h2>
    <p id="head-race">
      <strong>Head races</strong> are time trials, usually in the fall. Boats start one at a time,
      about 10 to 20 seconds apart, and race against the clock over a winding course of a few
      kilometres. You won't see who's winning from the bank. The order only becomes clear once every
      boat has finished.
    </p>
    <p id="sprint">
      <strong>Sprints</strong> are side-by-side races in lanes, usually in the spring, often over
      1,500 or 2,000 metres. Big events have heats, and the fastest crews move on to a final.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="boats">
    <h2>Boats and event names</h2>
    <p>
      The number is how many rowers are in the boat. A <strong>+</strong> means it carries a
      coxswain, a <strong>-</strong> means it doesn't, and an <strong>x</strong> means sculling,
      where each rower has two oars.
    </p>
    <dl>
      <dt>8+</dt>
      <dd>An eight: eight rowers and a coxswain.</dd>
      <dt>4+ / 4x</dt>
      <dd>A coxed four, or a quad of four scullers.</dd>
      <dt>2x / 1x</dt>
      <dd>A double and a single, both sculling.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p id="events">
      Event names add who can enter. <strong>U17</strong> and <strong>U19</strong> are age limits,{" "}
      <strong>Novice</strong> usually means first-year rowers, and <strong>Masters</strong> are
      adult rowers who race with an age handicap.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="seats">
    <h2>Who sits where</h2>
    <p>
      The <strong>coxswain</strong> steers and calls the race. Seats are numbered from the bow (the
      front) to the <strong>stroke</strong> seat at the back, nearest the coxswain. The stroke sets
      the rhythm for everyone else.
    </p>
    <p>Results only list the stroke's name, so that's the name CrewDash shows for each boat.</p>
    <p id="bow-number">
      The <strong>bow number</strong> is the number the regatta gives each boat. In a head race it
      also sets the starting order.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="results">
    <h2>Reading a result</h2>
    <dl>
      <dt id="place">Place, "of" field size</dt>
      <dd>
        "4th of 12" means fourth out of twelve crews in the event. Field sizes change a lot, so the
        place is always shown with the number of crews.
      </dd>
      <dt id="adjusted-time">Time</dt>
      <dd>
        The time shown is the <strong>adjusted time</strong>: the time on the water plus any
        penalties and minus any age handicap. Places are decided on adjusted time.
      </dd>
      <dt id="margin">Margin</dt>
      <dd>
        "+0:12.3" means 12.3 seconds behind the winner. Weather and water affect every crew in a
        race, so the margin is a fairer measure than the time alone.
      </dd>
      <dt id="gaps">Gaps and crews beaten</dt>
      <dd>
        "2.1 s behind 3rd" is how far the crew one place up finished ahead. "1.4 s off third" is the
        gap to a medal. Both come from adjusted times in the same race.
      </dd>
      <dt id="pace">Pace per 500 m</dt>
      <dd>
        The time on the water spread over each 500 metres of the course, the unit rowers train in.
        It only shows when the venue's course length is known, and it only compares fairly on the
        same course.
      </dd>
      <dt id="penalties">Penalties</dt>
      <dd>
        Seconds added for breaking a rule, such as hitting a buoy or getting in another crew's way.
      </dd>
      <dt id="handicap">Handicap</dt>
      <dd>
        Masters crews get seconds taken off for the crew's average age, so older crews can race
        younger ones fairly. CrewDash shows the time on the water ("raw time") and the handicap next
        to the adjusted time.
      </dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="exhibition">
    <h2>Exhibition boats</h2>
    <p>
      An exhibition boat races but can't win a place or a medal. It usually happens when a crew has
      to borrow a rower from another squad, for example a varsity rower filling in for a novice who
      is sick. The boat races to give the crew the experience.
    </p>
    <p id="would-have-placed">
      CrewDash shows where an exhibition boat <strong>would have placed</strong> on time, marked
      unofficial. It never changes the official results.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="statuses">
    <h2>When there's no place</h2>
    <dl>
      <dt>No result yet</dt>
      <dd>The boat hasn't finished, or the regatta hasn't posted it.</dd>
      <dt>Did not start / Scratched</dt>
      <dd>The boat didn't race.</dd>
      <dt>Did not finish</dt>
      <dd>The boat started but didn't cross the line.</dd>
      <dt>Disqualified</dt>
      <dd>The officials removed the result for a rule breach.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="muted">
      Results are sometimes corrected after race day. CrewDash picks up corrections, and alerts send
      an update when a result changes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="comparing">
    <h2>Comparing times</h2>
    <p>
      Every course is a different length, and wind and current change from day to day, so a time at
      one regatta says nothing about a time at another. CrewDash only compares times at the same
      regatta from year to year, and even then the conditions can differ.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="race-day">
    <h2>On race day</h2>
    <p>
      Race schedules shift, so arrive early and check results often. Bring layers, rain gear and a
      folding chair, because you may be outside all day. Keep off the docks and let crews focus
      while they launch. The best place to cheer is usually near the finish. Turn on{" "}
      <a href="/notifications">Alerts</a> to get a notification when our boats start and when each
      event's results are in.
    </p>
  </section>
  {(clubTips || chatUrl) && (
    <section class="card" id="club-tips">
      <h2>Tips from {club.name}</h2>
      {clubTips && <p class="tips">{clubTips}</p>}
      {chatUrl && (
        <p>
          Questions? Ask in the <a href={chatUrl}>club group chat</a>.
        </p>
      )}
    </section>
  )}
  <p class="card">
    Know a family who'd find this useful? <a href="/share">Send them an invite</a>, no password
    needed.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .tips {
    white-space: pre-line;
  }
  dl {
    margin: 8px 0 0;
  }
  dt {
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-top: 8px;
  }
  dd {
    margin: 2px 0 0;
  }
  :target {
    scroll-margin-top: 16px;
    outline: 2px solid var(--brand-3);
    outline-offset: 4px;
    border-radius: 6px;
  }
</style>
